<%- include('../partials/head') %>
<body class="ph-body">
  <%- include('../partials/nav') %>

  <section class="ph-page-hero">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <div class="ph-eyebrow">Guide · Extras</div>
      <h1 class="ph-page-title">Sharing, Widgets &amp; Notifications</h1>
      <p class="ph-page-lede">Deep links to friends, the share extension that subscribes from Safari, Home Screen widgets that play without opening the app, and notifications you actually want.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">Sharing podcasts and episodes</h2>
      <ul class="ph-checklist ph-checklist-wide">
        <li><strong>Share an episode.</strong> Open the episode detail page, tap the share icon. Picks the standard iOS share sheet — Messages, Mail, Notes, anywhere.</li>
        <li><strong>Share a podcast.</strong> Same idea from a podcast detail page.</li>
        <li><strong>What gets shared</strong> is a clean web URL hosted at <em>artisanalsoftware.com/podhaven/podcast?…</em> or <em>/episode?…</em>. The page renders the podcast or episode for any browser, with a one-tap "Open in PodHaven" button for recipients who have the app.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="ph-tip">Recipients without PodHaven still see the show — they just won't have the deep-link button. The page is human-readable HTML with the description, audio player, and a link to the publisher.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section ph-section-alt">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">The share extension (subscribe from anywhere)</h2>
      <p class="ph-lede">Found a podcast in Safari, Mail, or any app? PodHaven registers a share extension you can use directly from the iOS share sheet.</p>
      <ol class="ph-steps">
        <li>Tap the share button in any app.</li>
        <li>Pick <strong>PodHaven</strong> from the actions list. (Scroll the action row, or hit <em>More</em> and enable PodHaven once.)</li>
        <li>The app opens, parses the URL, and offers to subscribe.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Works on Safari pages with podcast embeds, RSS feed URLs you paste, and PodHaven's own shared links from friends.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section">
    <div class="ph-container ph-feature">
      <div class="ph-feature-copy">
        <h2>Home Screen widgets</h2>
        <p>PodHaven ships <strong>Home Screen widgets</strong> with playback intents — meaning the widgets aren't just dashboards, they're interactive. You can play, pause, skip, and jump to the next track <em>without opening the app</em>.</p>
        <p>The Now Playing widget shows your current episode's artwork, title, podcast name, publish date, and remaining duration. Below that, four live controls: skip-back by your interval, play / pause, skip-forward by your interval, and next-track.</p>
        <ol class="ph-steps">
          <li>Long-press any empty area on your Home Screen.</li>
          <li>Tap <strong>Edit</strong> → <strong>Add Widget</strong>.</li>
          <li>Search for <strong>PodHaven</strong>.</li>
          <li>Pick a size and place it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The widget reflects play / pause state in real time. Tap the artwork or title area to launch into the episode in PodHaven; tap a control to act on playback in place.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="ph-feature-shot ph-feature-shot-landscape">
        <img src="/public/images/podhaven/widget-now-playing.png" alt="The PodHaven Now Playing widget on the Home Screen, showing the current episode with artwork, title, podcast, date, duration, and four interactive playback controls." loading="lazy">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section ph-section-alt">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">New-episode notifications</h2>
      <p class="ph-lede">PodHaven's notifications are <strong>off by default</strong> per podcast — and that's the right default. Turn them on only for the shows you'd want a ping for.</p>

      <h3>One-time setup</h3>
      <ol class="ph-steps">
        <li>The first time you turn on per-podcast notifications, iOS asks for permission. Allow it.</li>
        <li>If you previously denied, go to <em>iOS Settings → PodHaven → Notifications</em> and toggle <em>Allow Notifications</em> back on. The per-podcast settings sheet shows you a one-tap link to that screen when needed.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Per podcast</h3>
      <ol class="ph-steps">
        <li>Open the podcast you want pings for.</li>
        <li>Tap the settings icon in the top toolbar.</li>
        <li>Under <strong>Notifications</strong>, toggle <em>Notify New Episodes</em> on.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Notifications include the episode artwork as an attachment and deep-link straight to the episode in PodHaven when tapped. They fire during background feed refresh, so they arrive while the app is closed.</p>

      <div class="ph-tip">
        <strong>Recommendation:</strong> turn notifications on for two or three shows where missing an episode would actually be a loss. Turn them off everywhere else. The daily news show that already auto-queues to the top probably doesn't need a notification.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">Background refresh</h2>
      <p>Independent of notifications, PodHaven uses iOS's background refresh API to update feeds quietly while the app is in the background. This is what makes new episodes appear ready when you open the app, instead of needing a manual pull-to-refresh.</p>
      <p>You don't need to configure anything. Background refresh is governed by iOS's own scheduling — generally tracking how often you open the app and how heavy the device is on battery / network at the moment.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section ph-section-alt">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">Lock-screen, CarPlay, AirPods, Watch</h2>
      <p>PodHaven uses iOS's long-form audio session, so the lock-screen, control center, CarPlay UI, AirPods squeeze, and Apple Watch playback controls all reflect the same playhead, the same artwork, and the same chapter info as the in-app player.</p>
      <p>Pause on your phone, resume on CarPlay, finish on AirPods. The position is synced through the system, so there's no "wait a sec, where was I?" beat.</p>
      <p>What your <strong>Next</strong> button does on those surfaces is configurable in <em>Settings → Playback → Next Track Behavior</em>: jump to the next episode, skip by your interval, or move to the next chapter (when chapters exist).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%- include('../partials/footer') %>
